      "critique": "Input: Pass. This is a plausible dictated 43-step procedure with recoverable intent, explicit thresholds, and additional stakeholder requirements. Its length is substantive rather than padding. Output: Fail. Literal backslash-n sequences appear instead of actual line breaks, leaving the numbered procedure improperly formatted. Pair: Fail. The rewrite substantially redesigns the procedure rather than polishing it. Steps 2–7 omit the healthy offsite WAL shipping/zero-lag check, replace hash partitioning across user identifiers with monthly range partitioning on transaction timestamps, omit non-locking DDL and nullable shadow columns, and invent migration_plan.sql plus 24 months of historical and 12 months of future partitions. The dual-write deployment requirement is narrowed to inserts in the data access layer; the Datadog check requiring write latency within 2% of baseline disappears. Backfill no longer explicitly uses parallel batches of 5,000 rows per worker thread and instead adds primary-key batching. Steps 21–28 replace the PgBouncer 60-second server idle timeout, logical replication lag under 10 milliseconds, upcoming-fiscal-quarter partition allocation, disk write queues below five operations, pg-statistic histogram checks, foreign-key integrity without performance degradation, logging queries over 200 milliseconds, and WAL archival within two minutes to geo-redundant storage. Their replacements introduce unrelated requirements such as write amplification below 3, idle timeouts below 30 seconds, row-count comparisons, cache hits above 85%, near-zero lock contention, and point-in-time archive checks. The foreign-key check retains only part of the original requirement. Steps 36–43 omit CPU below 50% under peak analytical workloads, zero-lag secondary standby processing, restoration dry runs under 45 minutes, index fragmentation below 5%, principal database architect approval and Jira archival, midnight statistics generation, and confirmation that 100% of application read/write traffic has moved to the new tables. They instead add post-backfill ANALYZE, WAL throughput monitoring, cross-region sub-second replication, error rates below 0.01%, a 500-millisecond query threshold, security scans, dashboard archival, and database operations lead sign-off. The audit-logging replacement also drops secure audit archives. Although steps 8–20, 29–35, and the intervening stakeholder paragraph largely preserve the supplied material, these extensive omissions, altered thresholds, changed authorities, and invented obligations violate /polish.",
